<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Gulistan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1813">1813</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War (1804–1813)</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Gulistan was signed in October 1813 between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1804-1813">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1804–1813. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> recognised Russian possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and most of the khanates of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a>, and granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> exclusive naval rights on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>. Disputes over its vague borders led to a new war in 1826 and to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan.jpg" alt="Treaty of Gulistan" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Siamax Original uploader was Kaveh at fa.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gulistan-Treaty.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-russia">Alexander I</a> became tsar in 1801, eager to expand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s control over its neighbours. In Iran Fath Ali Shah <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qajar-dynasty">Qajar</a> had become shah after the assassination of his uncle Agha Mohammad Khan in 1797. Agha Mohammad Khan had re-subordinated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> and subjects in what are now eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>. After his death and that of Heraclius II of Georgia, Russia, seeking territory and trade, took the chance to annex eastern Georgia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>'s primary motive in entering the war was to reassert control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> and protect its north-western borders. Its army was as much as five times larger than the Russian force in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> but lagged behind technologically and was poorly trained. The Persian court declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> for aid, but after the Treaty of Tilsit in 1807 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> left Persia unassisted. The turning point was the Battle of Aslanduz on 31 October 1812, a decisive Persian defeat.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty was concluded on 24 October 1813 in the village of Gulistan. The negotiations had been precipitated by General Pyotr Kotlyarevsky's storming of Lankaran on 1 January 1813. The text was prepared by the British diplomat Sir Gore Ouseley, who acted as mediator, and signed by Nikolai Rtischev for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and Mirza Abolhassan Khan Ilchi for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> was confirmed in possession of the khanates of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a>, Ganja, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shaki-azerbaijan">Shaki</a>, Shirvan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quba">Quba</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baku">Baku</a>, with part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/talysh">Talysh</a> and the fortress of Lankaran. Persia abandoned its claims to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dagestan">Dagestan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, Mingrelia, Imeretia and Abkhazia. Iran lost the right to navigate the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caspian-sea">Caspian Sea</a>, and Russia received exclusive rights to station a military fleet there. Free trade was agreed, with a 5% duty on imports on both sides, and Russia promised to support <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-mirza">Abbas Mirza</a> as heir to the Persian throne.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The treaty did not settle whether the Persian army would be disarmed or allowed to regroup, and both sides saw that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> might try to reclaim the territories. The costs of the war nevertheless led to relative peace from 1813 to 1826, and permanent diplomatic missions were set up in both countries to keep trade open. The treaty contributed directly to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-persian-war-1826-1828">Russo-Persian War</a> of 1826 to 1828, which ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-turkmenchay">Treaty of Turkmenchay</a>, by which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> lost its last Caucasian territories.</p><p>Iranians regard Gulistan and Turkmenchay as among the most humiliating treaties ever signed, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fath-ali-shah-qajar">Fath Ali Shah</a> is judged for it as one of the most incompetent rulers in memory. Imperial Russian historians argued that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>'s absorption of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-caucasus">Transcaucasia</a> shielded local populations from Iranian and Ottoman invasions. The ceded territories came under Russian, and later Soviet, control for about 180 years.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Treaty of Gulistan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Treaty of Gulistan</b> of 1813 and the Treaty of Turkmenchay of 1828 it lost Georgia, Armenia and its Caspian navy to Russia and paid an indemnity of three million pounds to the tsar; in the Treaty of Paris of 1857 it agreed to withdraw from Herat.</blockquote><a href="/movement/persian-constitutional-revolution">Persian Constitutional Revolution · Background</a></li><li><blockquote>The Russian Empire established a protectorate in 1806 and annexed Tabasaran in 1813, recognised in the <b>Treaty of Gulistan</b> with Persia.</blockquote><a href="/state/tabasaran-maysumate">Tabasaran Maysumate</a></li><li><blockquote>Russian forces stormed Lankaran on 1 January 1813, and the <b>Treaty of Gulistan</b> later that year confirmed Russian protection.</blockquote><a href="/state/talysh-khanate">Talysh Khanate</a></li></ul></section>
